>
  <p>Traverse is one orchestrator binary — <code>runtime.wasm</code> — shared across hosts that have actually shipped. WASM is how the sandbox and portable artifacts work. It is not the product identity, and it is not a license to claim every OS and cloud placement.</p>

  <h2>Embedders are clients</h2>
  <p>The browser package, native Rust embedder, CLI, and MCP server load and talk to the same runtime model. They do not each reinvent a separate “Traverse runtime” in their language. When you call a capability from npm, crates.io, or an MCP client, you are asking that shared runtime to discover → execute → trace against a known contract.</p>

  <h2>Honest consumers today</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>JS/TS</strong> — published npm <code>traverse-embedder-web@0.12.0</code></li>
    <li><strong>Rust</strong> — published crates.io <code>traverse-embedder@0.12.0</code></li>
    <li><strong>AI agents</strong> — published <code>traverse-mcp</code> (stdio bootstrap)</li>
    <li><strong>Python</strong> — works by shelling out to <code>traverse-cli capability-package execute</code>. There is <strong>no Python SDK</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Swift / Kotlin / .NET</strong> — exist in-tree and are usable for experimentation; they are not first-class public package releases yet.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Edge is planned. Cloud placement is an explicit non-goal for v0.1. Before assuming a host works, read <a href="/platforms.html">Platforms</a> and <a href="/what-is-real-today.html">what is real today</a>.</p>

  <h2>What this is not</h2>
  <p>It is not “write once, run on any OS forever.” It is one governed capability on the hosts Traverse has actually shipped. Capability WASM and the shared runtime are different layers: your business rule is a capability artifact; the orchestrator that validates and runs it is <code>runtime.wasm</code>.</p>
